body:
@vaner-org Alright, I've sent https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/112450. This makes it so that all you need to do is the following.

```cpp
	bool is_abort = cur_request == ONE_SHOT_REQUEST_ABORT;
	if (is_reset && (is_fading_out || abort_on_reset)) {
		is_abort = true;
	}
```

> If the original behavior changing was indeed a bug and not intended like I thought when I wrote the issue, I do think that making true the default setting should be considered, since it is antithetical to the point of using a oneshot that it reset after interruption. That the change that 4.3 brought wasn't noticed until now should be evidence for that, I'd argue that it's worth making a breaking change here.

As explained in #112450, it has been the case that Abort is executed when reset occurs "during Fadeout" as existed behavior. This option is synonymous with an option to always perform that Abort regardless of fading state.

Regarding break compat in #112450, we have already suggested that users explicitly configure to avoid reset. Then I believe we should avoid adding further steps on that with this PR. Therefore, I conclude that the default should be `false` for now.

Thank you for merging, the updated and squashed commit is ready.

I'd like to reiterate that the problem in the original issue was the need for the ability to abort upon an interruption, not always abort a oneshot if reset is enabled - that would make it impossible to arm a oneshot on any resetting entrypoint. I only need the oneshot's arming to be good for exactly one trigger and no more.

For that reason, the condition has to be...
```
if (is_reset && (is_fading_out || (abort_on_reset && cur_active))) {
	is_abort = true;
}
```

I've already explained this in the tooltip for "Abort on Reset," but let me know if the name itself is unclear. That being said, I cannot imagine a single use case for the alternative, so I really doubt it needs clarification.
